# How to fix error E0499: cannot borrow as mutable more than once

Short answer: two `&mut` borrows of the same value were alive at the same time. A value can have exactly one mutable borrow at a time. End the first borrow before the second one starts — under modern Rust that usually just means reordering the code so each borrow is used and done — or restructure so the borrows point at different memory.

## The error

fn main() {

 let mut scores = vec! [10, 20, 30];

 let a = &mut scores;

 let b = &mut scores;

 a. push(40);

 b. push(50);

 println!("{a:?} {b:?}");

}

▶ Run this in the Rust Playground

Compiling that with `cargo build` prints:

error[E0499]: cannot borrow `scores` as mutable more than once at a time

 --> src/main.rs:4:13

 |

3 | let a = &mut scores;

 | ----------- first mutable borrow occurs here

4 | let b = &mut scores;

 | ^^^^^^^^^^^ second mutable borrow occurs here

5 | a.push(40);

 | - first borrow later used here



For more information about this error, try `rustc --explain E0499`.

`a` borrows `scores` mutably, and because `a` is used after `b` is created, the compiler treats both borrows as alive at once. Rust does not allow that: two write handles to the same `Vec` could disagree about the vector’s length, and one of them could hand out a reference to memory the other just freed.

